//@version=6
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  RIPTIDE UNDERTOW — Structure Bias & Pin Continuation
//
//  An undertow is the current beneath the surface that drags you back out. That
//  is the whole thesis: the pullback is the surface move, the trend is the
//  current under it, and a setup exists when the surface fails and the undertow
//  takes price back.
//
//  WHAT IT DOES, in one pass:
//    1. reads the trend from major/minor market structure, and refuses to trade
//       a trend that is ENDING
//    2. finds the counter-trend-coloured pin at the pullback extreme — green in
//       a downtrend, red in an uptrend
//    3. takes that candle's high, low and open as Working, Failure and Focus
//    4. waits for a close beyond BOTH the high and the low, in either order,
//       inside a bar window
//    5. draws a limit at the Focus line, a stop beyond the pullback, and a 3R
//       target
//
//  NOTHING HERE IS MEASURED AND NOTHING HERE IS A TRADE. This is v1 of a bench:
//  it draws setups so the DETECTION can be judged by eye and fixed. There is no
//  win rate, no R total, no stats table and no alert, because none of that
//  would mean anything until the detection is right. The design, the defaults
//  and every open question are in ../SPEC.md; the two nearest measurements in
//  this project — both negative — are named in ../INDICATOR.md.
//
//  SECTION 3 IS A COPY, AND NOT A VERBATIM ONE. The market-structure engine is
//  section 12 of riptide-indicator-v2.pine with the DRAWING removed and each
//  condition hoisted into a named bool so it can also be counted. Pine cannot
//  import, so a copy is the only option, and a copy is free to drift.
//
//  deploy/undertow-ms-check.py holds it: it extracts every engine condition and
//  assignment from both files, ignores anything that only draws, and reports
//  any statement present in one and not the other — in EITHER direction, since
//  the likelier drift is a fix landing in v2 that never reaches here. It runs
//  inside deploy/preflight.py. That check is the only thing standing between
//  this and two charts quietly disagreeing about what a CHoCH is.
//
//  SECTION 4 IS NOT A COPY. v2 has no minor-structure state machine; it uses
//  the short swings only for the inducement. Section 4 is Undertow's own and
//  the parity check does not cover it.
//
//  REPAINT, stated plainly. The structure engine reads the CURRENT forming bar,
//  exactly as it does in v2, so its labels can move intrabar and settle at the
//  close. Everything Undertow itself decides — the pin, the confirmations, the
//  fill — is gated on `barstate.isconfirmed`, so a setup never appears and then
//  vanishes. The swing dots lag by their detection period and are plotted back
//  in time; they do not repaint.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// `max_bars_back` IS NOT COSMETIC HERE. Two places index a series by a value
// Pine cannot see at compile time: `high[size]` in the structure engine, where
// size is an input up to 200, and `obPH[b]` in the order-block scan, where b
// runs to 300. Without a declared referencing length Pine infers one from the
// largest CONSTANT offset in the file, finds it far too small, and raises AT
// RUNTIME -- which does not disable one feature, it kills the whole script.
// That is what "enabling order blocks hides the stats table and the trades"
// was: not a drawing budget, an unhandled error taking the indicator with it.
indicator("Riptide Undertow — Structure Bias & Pin Continuation", "Undertow",
  overlay = true, max_lines_count = 500, max_labels_count = 500,
  max_boxes_count = 500, max_bars_back = 500)


// ═════════════════════════════ 1. INPUTS ═════════════════════════════════════
// Six groups, ~25 inputs. Every tooltip says what the setting does to the
// COUNT, because on a detector that is the only thing any of them decides.

string gBias = "1 · Bias"
string gPin = "2 · Candle"
string gSet = "3 · Setup"
string gLvl = "4 · Levels"
string gShow = "5 · Display"
string gDbg = "6 · Debug"

// TWO LENGTHS, WHICH IS THE WHOLE STRUCTURE PANEL NOW. It was seven inputs: a
// swing-source dropdown, three "price move" dials, two bar-pivot lengths and
// an inducement toggle. The engine underneath is LuxAlgo's Smart Money
// Concepts (section 3), which has one detector and two scales.
smcSwingLen = input.int(14, "Swing structure", minval = 10, maxval = 200,
  group = gBias,
  tooltip = "THE MAJOR TIER, and the bias every setup is taken with. A pivot confirmed by this many bars either side — It ships at 14. LuxAlgo's own default is 50, which on 15m is twelve and a half hours and leaves the bias tradeable only 16% of the time.\n\nIT IS THE LARGEST SINGLE LEVER ON THIS CHART. The old engine ran 6 here. At 50 the bias flips far less often and there are far fewer setups, which is the change you asked for and not a side effect.\n\nNOT MEASURED AT THIS VALUE. Every page in ../measurements was produced on the previous engine at 6/2. ../measurements/UNDERTOW_V2.md scored the ENGINE swap at +0.002 / +0.065 / +0.006 R per trade — the detector is the same expression either way — but it did not score this chart's configuration.")
smcInternalLen = input.int(5, "Internal structure", minval = 2, maxval = 50,
  group = gBias,
  tooltip = "THE MINOR TIER, same engine at a shorter length. It drives the inducement, the minor CHoCH that ends a trend, and the 'minor swing' stop source.\n\nLuxAlgo's default is 5; the old engine used 2.")
endMinor = input.string("on the flip", "End: minor structure", group = gBias,
  options = ["off", "on the flip", "while opposed"],
  tooltip = "The short swings turn before the long ones. 'on the flip' fires the bar the minor structure turns against the trend and can be missed if it turned earlier; 'while opposed' is true the whole time it is against, so it catches far more and leaves far fewer setups.")
retraceMax = input.int(70, "End: retraced % of the impulse", minval = 0, maxval = 100, group = gBias,
  tooltip = "Give back this much of the move since the CHoCH and the trend is over in any practical sense. 0 turns it off. THE STRONGEST CHOP GUARD HERE and it needs no extra indicator.")
// THREE ENDING RULES CAME OFF THE CHART. `endSweep`, `endStale` with its
// `staleBars`, and the ADX gate `adxMin` all shipped OFF, none was ever
// measured switched ON, and four inputs offering settings nothing supports is
// the habit ../SETTINGS.md was written to stop. They stay in the port because
// undertow_v2 and undertow_mtf_default name them, and the port is where an
// unmeasured option belongs until it is measured.

wickEdge = input.float(0.05, "Wick edge", minval = 0.0, maxval = 0.5, step = 0.01, group = gPin,
  tooltip = "How much the dominant wick must beat the other, as a fraction of the bar's range. THIS IS THE DOJI FILTER. 0 accepts near-balanced bars.")
useHammer = input.bool(true, "Hammer family (lower wick)", group = gPin,
  tooltip = "Hammer and hanging man. Off halves the candidates.")
useStar = input.bool(true, "Star family (upper wick)", group = gPin,
  tooltip = "Shooting star and inverted hammer. Off halves the candidates.")
famStrict = input.bool(false, "Priority shape only", group = gPin,
  tooltip = "ONLY THE SHOOTING STAR IN A BULL TREND, ONLY THE HAMMER IN A BEAR TREND. The hanging man and the inverted hammer stop being setups at all.\n\nThe chart already ranks the two shapes — the priority one wins when both are live, and the second choice trades when the priority one is absent. This turns the ranking into a GATE: the second choice never trades.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT IS EXACTLY ONE CODE PER DIRECTION. The colour rule already fixes the candle's colour, so strict + colour is SS in a bull trend and HAM in a bear trend, and the four-code taxonomy collapses to two.\n\nIT COSTS 42–46% OF THE ARMED SETUPS, measured on the 23-symbol set at 15m / 30m / 1h. The two surviving codes are untouched — 474 shooting stars become 475, 453 hammers become 454 — so nothing is being reshuffled: the cut is the hanging man and the inverted hammer leaving.\n\nOFF AND UNMEASURED, and ../measurements/UNDERTOW_ANCHOR.md is the reason to be careful with this one in particular. That study took the author's stated anchor, confirmed it found the intended candle 86–88% of the time, and the trades were WORSE. A shape filter is the same kind of claim: clearly stated, mechanically correct, and not yet shown to make money. It gets a pre-registration before it becomes the default.")

string tTouch = "close at or beyond"
string tBody = "whole body beyond"
// "A CLOSE BEYOND" IS THE RULE, not a preference, so it is no longer two
// dropdowns. `workTest` and `failTest` offered three variants each; nobody can
// judge "close at or beyond" against "whole body beyond" from a chart and no
// study has ever varied them. They stay in the port, unmeasured, where an
// unmeasured option belongs.
// THE TWO TESTS ARE NO LONGER THE SAME, and that is the strategy's author's
// correction: "after exact close AT the failure we will consider as failed, at
// that failure or beyond". A close that lands exactly on the Failure line IS
// the failure. Working stays strict — it has to be genuinely cleared for the
// counter-trend attempt to count as having worked.
//
// It matters less often than it sounds and more than nothing: an exact equality
// needs a close on the tick, which happens on coarse ticks and round numbers.
// It is here because it is the rule, not because it was measured.
string kWork = "close beyond"
string kFail = tTouch
confirmOrder = input.string("working then failure", "Confirmations", group = gSet,
  options = ["working then failure", "either order"],
  tooltip = "THE ORDER THE TWO CONFIRMATIONS MUST ARRIVE IN, and the default is a CORRECTION rather than a preference.\n\n'working then failure' is the strategy: the pin is a counter-trend candle, W is it appearing to WORK as a reversal, F is that reversal FAILING, and the limit goes at the Focus line on the failure. So the attempt has to happen first.\n\n    W → F          arms\n    F → W → F      arms, on the second F\n    F → W          does NOT arm — waits for an F\n\n'either order' is what v1 did and it is a different event: it admits bars where the reversal never worked at all and only the same two lines were touched. Every measurement page in ../measurements was produced under it.\n\nA bar that spans BOTH lines does not arm either way — intrabar order is unknowable and this indicator resolves that against the trade everywhere else.")
confirmBars = input.int(20, "Confirm within", minval = 2, maxval = 200, group = gSet,
  tooltip = "Bars for BOTH confirmations to land. Without a bound the rule is eventually true for almost any candle.")
fillBars = input.int(20, "Fill within", minval = 1, maxval = 200, group = gSet,
  tooltip = "Bars for the limit to fill before the setup expires unfilled.")
armWins = input.bool(false, "First to confirm wins", group = gSet,
  tooltip = "WHEN ONE CANDIDATE COMPLETES ITS W→F, DROP THE OTHERS in the same direction that have not.\n\nA pullback offers several counter-trend candles. 'Newest wins' already thins them as new ones appear, but once one has actually completed the pattern the rest are still sitting there waiting to arm on their own levels — which is how four limit orders end up resting inside one pullback, with their lines on top of each other.\n\nIT IS A BETTER SELECTOR THAN THE LIVE CAP because it selects on an EVENT rather than on age. The cap keeps the oldest four and turns away everything after; this keeps the one that completed the pattern. Measured on a spent universe at the cap of 4, it cuts cap-refusals from 511 to 364.\n\nAn already-ARMED setup is never dropped: it has levels and an order behind it.\n\nOFF AND UNMEASURED. It costs about 10% of armed setups — small, but still a change to which trades exist, and ../measurements/UNDERTOW_ANCHOR.md is what set the standard that those get a prereg rather than shipping because they are clearly stated.")
maxLive = input.int(4, "Live setups at once", minval = 1, maxval = 8, group = gSet,
  tooltip = "A pullback contains several pins and any of them might be the one that works, so they all run independently. This caps how many, for the chart and the drawing budget; pins arriving at the cap are turned away and counted.")
// The two anchors, named once. Both spellings are the PORT's constants
// verbatim — undertow-port-check.py compares dropdown strings by value, and a
// reworded option makes every branch behind it fall through in silence.
string sAnchPull = "pullback extreme"
string sAnchTrend = "trend extreme"
string sAnchLeg = "leg extreme"
pinAt = input.string("pullback extreme", "Anchor the pin at", group = gSet,
  options = ["pullback extreme", "trend extreme", "leg extreme"],
  tooltip = "WHERE THE COUNTER-TREND CANDLE IS LOOKED FOR, and the two options are different readings of the same rule — see ../SPEC.md 2.3d.\n\n'pullback extreme' is what v1 and v2 do and what every measurement page was produced under: the pin is the highest high of the rally away from the leg low.\n\n'trend extreme' is the strategy as its author states it: the pin is the BOUNCE ATTEMPT at the leg low itself. The stated priority shape in a downtrend is the hammer, which has a long LOWER wick, and a long lower wick at the TOP of a rally is not a rejection of anything.\n\n'leg extreme' is the same idea attached to the RIGHT OBJECT. 'trend extreme' anchors on the running extreme since the major CHoCH — ONE point per trend — and at a 50-bar swing the pin sits a median of 47 bars past it, so tolerance 0 keeps 3.9% of them. 'leg extreme' resets on every INTERNAL turn, so there is one anchor per minor leg, which is what the strategy's diagram shows: median 8 bars.\n\nIT IS NOT THE DEFAULT AND IT IS NOT PROMOTED. ../measurements/UNDERTOW_V3.md measured it on 45 unseen symbols: +0.023 / +0.023 / +0.054 R per trade against v1, positive on 3 of 3 and inside one standard error on 3 of 3. Not actively harmful, not an edge — which is exactly why it is selectable rather than on.")
locTol = input.int(0, "Anchor tolerance", minval = 0, maxval = 10, group = gSet,
  tooltip = "Bars a pin may sit PAST the anchor above and still count. 0 means it must BE the anchor bar — so if that bar is a doji or the wrong colour, the whole pullback is discarded and a textbook pin on the next bar, one tick lower, cannot qualify. That is defect 3 in ../SPEC.md 2.3b.\n\nIT MATTERS MOST ON THE OTHER ANCHOR. At 'trend extreme' with tolerance 0 the pinned bar IS the bar that made the leg low, so 75.6% of pins are the priority shape — a green bar whose own low is the low of the leg is a hammer by construction. One bar of tolerance takes that to 53.2% and two takes it to 51.5%, which is what an unfiltered mix looks like. ../measurements/UNDERTOW_V3.md has the table; the share is a description of the anchor, not a result.\n\nMEASURED at 0 and at 2 (../measurements/UNDERTOW_PULLBACK.md: the 2nd and 3rd candle score within ±0.025 R of the 1st) and at 50, which is the ablation's destroy-it arm rather than a tolerance.")
// THE TWO PULLBACK MINIMUMS CAME OFF THE CHART. `pbMinAge` and `pbMinDepth`
// were added to fix defects 1 and 2 in ../SPEC.md 2.3b, then measured:
// ../measurements/UNDERTOW_PULLBACK.md found the setups they remove are NOT
// systematically worse (+0.055 / -0.015 / -0.065) and that `pbMinAge` keeps a
// different share of setups on every timeframe. Both shipped at 0, meaning
// off, and two inputs that only ever remove trades that are no worse are two
// inputs nobody should be turning up. Still in the port for the study.

string sPin = "Pin high / low"
string sPull = "Pullback extreme"
string sSwing = "Minor swing extreme"
stopSrc = input.string("Pullback extreme", "Stop", group = gLvl,
  options = ["Pin high / low", "Pullback extreme", "Minor swing extreme"],
  tooltip = "Where the stop sits. Tighter stops mean bigger R and more setups stopped before the fill.")
stopTrack = input.bool(true, "Stop follows the pullback", group = gLvl,
  tooltip = "Arming a long REQUIRES a close below the pin's low, so a setup always arms while the pullback is still deepening and its stop is set on an unfinished move. On keeps the stop at the running extreme until the fill. You hold no position yet, so nothing is lost by moving it — but risk grows, the target moves with it, and 'stop taken before the fill' becomes impossible by construction.")
stopBuf = input.float(0.25, "Stop buffer, ATR", minval = 0.0, maxval = 3.0, step = 0.05, group = gLvl,
  tooltip = "Beyond the extreme, so a one-tick undercut does not take it. 0.25 is the buffer the CCP entry model used. 0 puts the stop exactly on the extreme.")
rr = input.float(3.5, "Reward ratio", minval = 0.5, maxval = 20.0, step = 0.5, group = gLvl,
  tooltip = "Target as a multiple of risk. Drawing only — nothing here scores an outcome.")
feeFrac = input.float(0.0007, "Round-trip fee", minval = 0.0, maxval = 0.01, step = 0.0001, group = gLvl,
  tooltip = "Round-trip cost as a fraction of NOTIONAL — 0.0007 is a maker-in / taker-out round trip on a major perp, and it is what every study in ../measurements used. It is not a guess at slippage, which is separate and worse.\n\nIT CHANGES THE PANEL, NOT THE STRATEGY. No level, entry or exit moves; what moves is `net R` and the break-even line beside the win rate.\n\nAND THE LINE MOVES MORE THAN IT LOOKS. The fee is charged in PRICE and the trade is scored in R, so the drag per trade is fee ÷ (risk as a fraction of entry). Risk runs about 1.3% of entry on 15m and 2.4% on 1h, so the same 7bp costs 0.055R there and 0.029R here, and break-even at 3.5R is 23.5% rather than 22.2%. ../measurements/UNDERTOW_V3.md found this by measuring a rule whose win rate sat above 22.2% on all three timeframes and was still making nothing.\n\nSet it to 0 to read the panel gross. That is what this indicator did until now, and a 23.0% win rate showed green.")

// THE BACKUP FILL CAME OFF THE CHART, eight inputs for a feature two studies
// found worthless. "if we miss we can fill the order as a backup on any OB or
// FVG" was a real idea and it was measured twice:
//
//   UNDERTOW_BACKUP_FILL.md   +0.03 R per armed setup, positive on 3 of 3 and
//                             significant on NONE -- two large, individually
//                             significant halves that nearly cancel
//   UNDERTOW_LATE_BACKUP.md   waiting for the Focus limit to expire removes
//                             the tax AND all the opportunity: 100% of the
//                             +0.6 R trades fill inside the window
//
// It shipped OFF and stayed off. The machinery is still in the port, where
// undertow_backup.py and undertow_late_backup.py keep reproducing their pages.
// The chart draws OB and FVG ZONES under Display -- that is separate, and it
// stays.

overlapMode = input.string("collapse", "Overlapping setups", group = gShow,
  options = ["collapse", "draw every one"],
  tooltip = "WHAT TO DRAW WHEN A SETUP FILLS WHILE ANOTHER IN THE SAME DIRECTION IS STILL OPEN. This is a DRAWING setting and it changes no number on the panel — 'entered', 'won / lost' and 'net R' count every fill either way.\n\nIt exists because the overlap is real and large. Measured across 23 symbols: on 15m, 74% of filled trades are running at the same time as another in the same direction, up to 13 at once, and when they overlap they resolve THE SAME WAY 78% of the time. Four longs within $10 of each other is one idea at four times the size, not four trades.\n\n'collapse' draws the first of each group in full and gives its label a ×N count; the others keep their dashed entry line so the level is still visible, and lose the box and the price label. 'draw every one' is the old behaviour.\n\nCOLLAPSING IS COSMETIC. It does not stop the strategy taking the trades. If you want FEWER of them that is a rule change, it would alter every measured number, and it has not been measured.")
showZones = input.bool(true, "Risk / reward zones", group = gShow)
showUnfilled = input.bool(true, "Draw setups that never filled", group = gShow,
  tooltip = "Armed setups that were stopped or expired before the limit filled, drawn muted with an ×. Off keeps the chart clean; ON is how you check past detection without waiting for live ones.")
showStruct = input.bool(true, "Market structure", group = gShow,
  tooltip = "CHoCH, BOS and the swing dots, so the bias can be checked by eye. One toggle for all three.")
showBias = input.bool(true, "Bias badge", group = gShow,
  tooltip = "One cell in the BOTTOM RIGHT with the trend bias and its state, so the stats table can be switched off without losing the only line most people read off it.\n\nThe bias is the MAJOR tier — swing structure at the length set in group 1. It decides whether a setup can exist at all: no setup is taken while it reads `ending` or `none`, however good the candle looks.\n\nOrange is `ending`, and it is worth knowing how sticky that is. The retrace rule latches, and it only clears on a new CHoCH or a break of structure in the trend's direction — at a 50-bar swing those can be a hundred bars apart.")
showStats = input.bool(true, "Stats table", group = gShow,
  tooltip = "The panel top-right. Its first row is the BIAS — direction and state — so turning the rest off still leaves that if you re-enable it. Off draws nothing and costs nothing.")
showOB = input.bool(false, "Order blocks", group = gShow, inline = "z1",
  tooltip = "THE LAST UNMITIGATED ORDER BLOCKS EITHER SIDE, drawn as boxes to the right edge and removed when price trades through them. DRAWING ONLY — nothing here enters on one.\n\nThe block is found the way LuxAlgo's Smart Money Concepts finds it, not by the looser 'last opposite candle' rule this chart used before: when structure breaks, the span from the broken pivot to the breaking bar is scanned and the block is its most extreme bar — the highest high for a bearish break, the lowest low for a bullish one.\n\nAND THE EXTREME IS TAKEN FROM A FILTERED HIGH/LOW. On a bar whose range is 2x ATR(200) the high and the low are SWAPPED before the scan, so one violent candle cannot become the order block for a whole leg. That filter is the substance of the difference.\n\nOff by default, and the backup fill that used to ENTER on these is gone from the chart entirely: ../measurements/UNDERTOW_BACKUP_FILL.md measured it at +0.03 R per armed setup, positive on 3 of 3 and significant on none. A drawn zone is a description; entering on one was measured and is not worth it.")
colOBBull = input.color(color.new(#3179f5, 80), "", group = gShow, inline = "z1")
colOBBear = input.color(color.new(#f77c80, 80), "", group = gShow, inline = "z1")
showFVG = input.bool(false, "Fair value gaps", group = gShow,
  tooltip = "Three-bar imbalances, drawn as two half-boxes split at the midpoint and removed when price fills them — the same shape LuxAlgo's Smart Money Concepts draws.\n\nTHE MIDPOINT IS DRAWN, as the border where the two halves meet. It is the level worth seeing: a gap filled halfway is the one deciding whether it holds.\n\nCOLOURS ARE LuxAlgo's OWN green and red, fixed rather than offered as inputs — a gap and an order block are different objects and telling them apart at a glance is worth more than a matching palette.\n\nDrawing only.")
fvgExtend = input.int(5, "… extend zones by", minval = 0, maxval = 50, group = gShow,
  tooltip = "How many bars past the gap the box is drawn. Cosmetic.")
zoneAuto = input.bool(false, "… auto threshold", group = gShow,
  tooltip = "Filter out small gaps using the average bar range. OFF by default because a filter nothing has measured is a filter nobody should trust — and every zone filter measured in this project has failed.")
keepN = input.int(45, "Keep last N setups", minval = 1, maxval = 60, group = gShow,
  tooltip = "How far back setups stay drawn. TradingView caps total drawings at 500, so a high value on a busy chart silently loses the oldest.")
colLong = input.color(color.new(#089981, 0), "Long", group = gShow, inline = "c1")
colShort = input.color(color.new(#f23645, 0), "Short", group = gShow, inline = "c1")
colLine = input.color(color.new(#787b86, 0), "Lines", group = gShow, inline = "c1")

dbgOn = input.bool(false, "Debug marks", group = gDbg,
  tooltip = "A dot on every pin found, one line of why, and a funnel in the panel.")
dbgRejects = input.bool(true, "Show rejected pins", group = gDbg,
  tooltip = "Also mark bars that were never eligible:\n\n    red ✕     wrong colour for the bias — a green bar in an uptrend\n    grey ✕    the right candle, too far from the anchor\n\nWITHOUT this, debug marks only the candles the strategy is actually hunting: a blue circle where one was taken and an ORANGE circle where the candle was right and the bias was not tradeable. That pair is what answers 'I can see a textbook candle there and nothing happened'.\n\nTurn this on when the question is the other one — why a bar you expected to be a candidate never even got that far. It is noisy by design: in a bull trend every green bar with a wick earns a red cross.\n\nHover any mark for the full reason. Needs Debug marks on.")


// ═════════════════════════════ 2. HELPERS ════════════════════════════════════
// Declared first: Pine reads top-down, and a function or a type used before it
// is declared does not compile.

// "Beyond" in each direction, under the three comparison modes. A line at the
// bar's HIGH is cleared upward; a line at its LOW is cleared downward. Which of
// the two is Working and which is Failure depends on the family (section 6).
beyondUp(float lvl, string mode) =>
    mode == tTouch ? close >= lvl : mode == tBody ? math.min(open, close) > lvl : close > lvl

beyondDn(float lvl, string mode) =>
    mode == tTouch ? close <= lvl : mode == tBody ? math.max(open, close) < lvl : close < lvl


// THE TWO SWING DETECTORS THAT USED TO LIVE HERE ARE GONE. `priceSwings` — a
// reversal of k x the last N hours' range — and `msSwings`, the N-bar pivot
// copied from riptide-indicator-v2. Section 3 now runs LuxAlgo's `leg()`,
// which ../port/smc.py proved is the SAME EXPRESSION as msSwings, so nothing
// was lost in detection; what went is a second and third way of spelling it.
//
// Both are still in the Python port, because ../measurements/UNDERTOW_PARAMS.md
// and UNDERTOW_BIAS_SOURCE.md were produced on them and have to keep
// reproducing.

type Cand
    int    bar    = na
    float  hi     = na
    float  lo     = na
    float  focus  = na
    bool   workHi = false
    bool   workOk = false
    bool   failOk = false
    int    workBar = -1
    bool   armed  = false
    int    armBar = na
    bool   short  = false
    float  stop   = na
    float  target = na
    float  pbExt  = na
    int    order  = 0
    string tag    = ""
    string code   = ""
    string state  = ""
    // GHOST. An armed setup the bias gate cancelled, kept running anyway and
    // scored into its own column. It draws nothing, takes no live slot and
    // never touches the real totals — it exists only to answer whether the
    // gate that killed it was right to. See section 7.
    bool   ghost  = false

// ── the drawing buffers, and the two calls section 7 makes ───────────────────
var array<line> lnBuf = array.new<line>()
var array<box> bxBuf = array.new<box>()
var array<label> lbBuf = array.new<label>()

prune() =>
    while lnBuf.size() > keepN * 3
        line.delete(lnBuf.shift())
    while bxBuf.size() > keepN * 2
        box.delete(bxBuf.shift())
    while lbBuf.size() > keepN * 2
        label.delete(lbBuf.shift())

drawLine(int x1, int x2, float y, color c, string st) =>
    lnBuf.push(line.new(x1, y, x2, y, xloc = xloc.bar_index, color = c, style = st, width = 1))

// THE OVERLAP. A chart of ETH on 15m showed four LONGs inside ten dollars,
// boxes and labels piled on each other. That is not a drawing accident, it is
// what the strategy does: measured across 23 symbols, 74% of filled trades on
// 15m are open at the same time as another in the SAME DIRECTION, up to 13 at
// once, and those groups resolve the same way 78% of the time.
//
// So the first of a group draws in full and the rest draw small. `openN` and
// `compact` are counted AT THE CALL SITES in section 7 — the live-trade array
// is declared there, Pine will not let a function here reach forward to it,
// and a function may not assign to a global either, so no state is kept
// between calls. NOTHING HERE TOUCHES A COUNTER: the panel counts every fill.

// A FILLED setup. The entry marker is a LABEL rather than a plotshape because
// two candidates can fill on the same bar and a plotshape draws one; it also
// lets the marker sit at the entry PRICE and carry a hover.
drawFill(Cand c, int openN) =>
    color cc = c.short ? colShort : colLong
    string info = c.tag + "\nbias at the pin: " + c.state + "\nentry " + str.tostring(c.focus, format.mintick) + "\nstop  " + str.tostring(c.stop, format.mintick) + "\ntgt   " + str.tostring(c.target, format.mintick) + "\n" + str.tostring(bar_index - c.bar) + " bars from the pin · " + (c.order == 1 ? "W→F" : "F→W")
    if openN > 0
        // A FOLLOWER: its entry line, faint, and a small badge carrying the
        // size of the group it just joined. No box and no price label, which
        // is what was piling up. The badge counts rather than a lead label
        // being edited later, because a function may not write to a global and
        // a retained label id goes stale the moment prune() reaches it.
        drawLine(c.bar, bar_index, c.focus, color.new(cc, 50),
          line.style_dashed)
        lbBuf.push(label.new(bar_index, c.focus,
          (c.short ? "▼" : "▲") + " ×" + str.tostring(openN + 1),
          xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = label.style_label_left,
          color = color.new(cc, 55), textcolor = color.white,
          size = size.tiny,
          tooltip = str.tostring(openN + 1) + " SETUPS IN THIS DIRECTION ARE OPEN AT ONCE, and this is the newest. Every one is counted on the panel; they are drawn small so they do not bury the first.\n\nThey are not independent bets. Measured across 23 symbols, overlapping groups resolve THE SAME WAY 78% of the time on 15m — one idea at " + str.tostring(openN + 1) + "x the size, not " + str.tostring(openN + 1) + " trades.\n\n" + info))
        prune()
    else
        drawLine(c.bar, bar_index, c.focus, cc, line.style_dashed)
        drawLine(c.bar, bar_index, c.stop, colLine, line.style_dotted)
        drawLine(c.bar, bar_index, c.target, colLine, line.style_dotted)
        if showZones
            // math.max/min rather than positional: on a LONG the stop is BELOW
            // the entry, and a fixed order draws the box inverted.
            bxBuf.push(box.new(c.bar, math.max(c.stop, c.focus), bar_index,
              math.min(c.stop, c.focus), border_color = color.new(cc, 70),
              bgcolor = color.new(colShort, 92)))
            bxBuf.push(box.new(c.bar, math.max(c.focus, c.target), bar_index,
              math.min(c.focus, c.target), border_color = color.new(cc, 70),
              bgcolor = color.new(colLong, 92)))
        // THE CANDLE THE TRADE WAS BUILT FROM, named on the bar itself and
        // placed outside the pin so it never covers what it points at.
        lbBuf.push(label.new(c.bar, c.short ? c.hi : c.lo, c.code,
          xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = c.short ? label.style_label_down
          : label.style_label_up, color = color.new(cc, 35),
          textcolor = color.white, size = size.normal,
          tooltip = "the setup candle\n" + info))
        // "▲ LONG 3.5R" WAS A CHART THAT LIED. `rr` is the configured reward
        // ratio, drawn at the moment of FILL — it is a plan, not a result —
        // and it sat in a green label over trades that stopped out on the next
        // bar. The ✓ / ✕ from drawResult is the outcome and it is the only
        // thing here that may carry a number. This label says what it is: an
        // entry, and where.
        lbBuf.push(label.new(bar_index, c.focus,
          (c.short ? "▼ SHORT " : "▲ LONG ")
          + str.tostring(c.focus, format.mintick),
          xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = label.style_label_left,
          color = color.new(cc, 20), textcolor = color.white,
          size = size.normal, tooltip = info))
        prune()

// WHERE A FILLED TRADE ENDED. A tick or a cross on the bar that resolved it, so
// the won/lost counts on the panel can be audited against the chart rather than
// trusted. One label per filled setup.
// `compact` while OTHER trades in the same direction are still open. Four
// ✓ +3.5R at the same target within a few bars is four labels in one place, and
// a crowded group nearly always resolves together — 78% of the time, measured.
// So the one that empties the group gets the full label and the rest a bare
// glyph that still hovers. The panel's won / lost counts all of them.
drawResult(Cand c, bool won, bool compact) =>
    string tip = (won ? "target" : "stop") + " · " + c.code + " " + (c.short ? "short" : "long") + "\nentry " + str.tostring(c.focus, format.mintick) + "\n" + str.tostring(bar_index - c.bar) + " bars from the pin" + (compact ? "\n\nDrawn small because other trades in the same direction were still open when this one resolved. Every one is counted on the panel." : "")
    lbBuf.push(label.new(bar_index, won ? c.target : c.stop,
      compact ? (won ? "✓" : "✕")
      : (won ? "✓ +" + str.tostring(rr, "#.#") + "R" : "✕ −1R"),
      xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = label.style_label_left,
      color = color.new(won ? colLong : colShort, compact ? 70 : 45),
      textcolor = color.white, size = compact ? size.tiny : size.normal,
      tooltip = tip))
    prune()

// ARMED and then gone. Muted and dashed, so a setup that did not happen can
// never be mistaken at a glance for one that did.
drawMiss(Cand c, string why) =>
    string info = c.tag + "\nbias at the pin: " + c.state + "\n" + why + "\nentry " + str.tostring(c.focus, format.mintick) + "\nstop  " + str.tostring(c.stop, format.mintick)
    drawLine(c.bar, bar_index, c.focus, color.new(color.gray, 55), line.style_dashed)
    lbBuf.push(label.new(c.bar, c.short ? c.hi : c.lo, c.code,
      xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = c.short ? label.style_label_down
      : label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.gray, 75),
      textcolor = color.new(color.gray, 10), size = size.normal,
      tooltip = "setup candle, never filled\n" + info))
    lbBuf.push(label.new(bar_index, c.focus, "×", xloc = xloc.bar_index,
      style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(color.gray, 75),
      textcolor = color.new(color.gray, 10), size = size.normal,
      tooltip = info))
    prune()



// ═══════ 3. MARKET STRUCTURE — LuxAlgo Smart Money Concepts ══════════════════
// Transcribed from LuxAlgo's "Smart Money Concepts", CC BY-NC-SA 4.0,
// https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/ — the same source
// ../port/smc.py transcribes for the Python side.
//
// IT REPLACES THE COPY OF riptide-indicator-v2's ENGINE that used to be here,
// by decision rather than by measurement, and the honest version of what that
// buys is in ../port/smc.py's own header: compared on real candles, the two
// engines' SWING DETECTOR and CHoCH are already IDENTICAL —
//
//     LuxAlgo   high[size] > ta.highest(size)
//     riptide   highs[i-msL] > max(highs[i-msL+1 .. i])
//
// the same expression, pivot for pivot at sizes 5, 6, 15 and 50, and CHoCH bar
// for CHoCH bar. ../measurements/UNDERTOW_V2.md then scored the swap at
// +0.002 / +0.065 / +0.006 R per trade. So this is not a better detector.
//
// WHAT IT ACTUALLY CHANGES, and all three are real:
//
//   1. THE SCALE. 50 and 5, LuxAlgo's own lengths, against 6 and 2. A 50-bar
//      pivot on 15m is twelve and a half hours. The bias will flip far less
//      often and there will be far fewer setups.
//   2. WHAT A BOS IS. riptide's BOS was a close beyond the running extreme
//      since the CHoCH, gated on an inducement sweep. LuxAlgo's is a close
//      beyond the last PIVOT in the trend's own direction, with no inducement
//      anywhere in it. `msBosNeedsIdm` has no counterpart and is gone.
//   3. TWO TIERS BY CONSTRUCTION. Swing structure at 50 is the major bias;
//      internal structure at 5 is the minor. Undertow already split major from
//      minor, so the mapping is exact and nothing downstream moved.
//
// deploy/undertow-ms-check.py, which anchored the old copy to v2.pine, no
// longer has anything to compare and is retired from preflight for this file.

// @type    a pivot level, its predecessor, and whether price has taken it out
type Piv
    float cur     = na
    float last    = na
    bool  crossed = false
    int   bx      = na

var Piv pvHi = Piv.new()
var Piv pvLo = Piv.new()
var Piv inHi = Piv.new()
var Piv inLo = Piv.new()

// LuxAlgo's leg(): 0 is a bearish leg, 1 a bullish one. A change of leg IS a
// pivot, and the pivot sits `size` bars back — which is what makes it
// confirmed rather than predicted.
//
// CALLED FROM TWO PLACES ON PURPOSE. Pine gives each call site its own copy of
// a function's `var` state and its own ta.* history, so one definition serves
// both the swing tier and the internal tier without them sharing a leg.
lgCapture(simple int size, Piv ph, Piv pl) =>
    var int lg = 0
    bool nh = high[size] > ta.highest(size)
    bool nl = low[size] < ta.lowest(size)
    int was = lg
    if nh
        lg := 0
    else if nl
        lg := 1
    if lg != was
        if lg == 1
            pl.last := pl.cur
            pl.cur := low[size]
            pl.crossed := false
            pl.bx := bar_index[size]
        else
            ph.last := ph.cur
            ph.cur := high[size]
            ph.crossed := false
            ph.bx := bar_index[size]
    lg != was

// THE BREAK. `crossed` is one-shot per pivot: a level is taken out once, and
// until a new pivot replaces it the same level cannot fire again. ta.crossover
// compares against the PREVIOUS bar's level, which ../port/smc.py records as
// one of the two details that have to be transcribed exactly rather than
// tidied.
lgCross(Piv ph, Piv pl) =>
    bool xu = ta.crossover(close, ph.cur)
    bool xd = ta.crossunder(close, pl.cur)
    bool up = xu and not ph.crossed
    bool dn = xd and not pl.crossed
    if up
        ph.crossed := true
    if dn
        pl.crossed := true
    [up, dn]

lgCapture(smcSwingLen, pvHi, pvLo)
lgCapture(smcInternalLen, inHi, inLo)
[swUp, swDn] = lgCross(pvHi, pvLo)
[inUp, inDn] = lgCross(inHi, inLo)

// SWING STRUCTURE — the major bias.
var int msOs = 0
var bool msSeen = false
bool msChoch = false
bool msBosUp = false
bool msBosDn = false
if swUp
    msChoch := msSeen and msOs != 1
    msBosUp := not msChoch
    msOs := 1
    msSeen := true
if swDn
    msChoch := msSeen and msOs != 0
    msBosDn := not msChoch
    msOs := 0
    msSeen := true

// The levels the rest of the file reads, under the names it already used.
float msTopY = pvHi.cur
float msBtmY = pvLo.cur
int   msTopX = pvHi.bx
int   msBtmX = pvLo.bx
float msSTopY = inHi.cur
float msSBtmY = inLo.cur

// RUNNING EXTREMES SINCE THE CHoCH, and these stay Undertow's own. The
// pullback rule anchors on them and deploy/undertow-pullback-check.py holds
// the two copies of that rule together by term, so replacing the structure
// engine must not disturb them.
var float msMax = na
var float msMin = na
var int msMaxX = na
var int msMinX = na
if msChoch or na(msMax)
    msMax := high
    msMin := low
    msMaxX := bar_index
    msMinX := bar_index

// NO SWEEP CONCEPT IN THIS ENGINE, and that is stated rather than faked.
// riptide's engine had one and the `endSweep` rule read it; LuxAlgo's does
// not, ../port/smc.py says so in as many words, and inventing one here to
// keep a retired Ending rule company would make the two look comparable while
// not being.

msMax := math.max(high, msMax)
msMin := math.min(low, msMin)
if msMax > msMax[1]
    msMaxX := bar_index
if msMin < msMin[1]
    msMinX := bar_index


// ═══════════ 4. INTERNAL STRUCTURE — THE MINOR TIER, SAME ENGINE ═════════════
// LuxAlgo runs the identical state machine at length 5 and calls it internal
// structure. Undertow's minor tier used to be its own code because v2 had no
// equivalent; now both tiers come from one transcription, which is the part of
// this swap that is unambiguously an improvement — one engine, two lengths,
// no second implementation to drift.
// ITS OWN CHoCH AND BOS, tagged the same way the major tier's are: a break
// that turns the internal trend is a CHoCH, one that continues it is a BOS.
// LuxAlgo draws BOTH tiers and the internal one is most of what you see on a
// chart -- at length 5 it fires many times between two swing breaks, which is
// why their screenshot is dense and ours was two labels.
var int msSOs = 0
var bool msSSeen = false
bool inChoch = false
bool inBosUp = false
bool inBosDn = false
if inUp
    inChoch := msSSeen and msSOs != 1
    inBosUp := not inChoch
    msSOs := 1
    msSSeen := true
if inDn
    inChoch := msSSeen and msSOs != 0
    inBosDn := not inChoch
    msSOs := 0
    msSSeen := true

bool msMinorChoch = msSOs != msSOs[1]

// THE PER-LEG RUNNING EXTREME, for the "leg extreme" anchor. Reset on every
// turn of the INTERNAL direction, so there is one of these per minor leg
// rather than one per trend — and unlike a confirmed swing it is known at the
// bar's own close, with no pivot-confirmation lag.
var float legMax = na
var float legMin = na
var int legMaxX = 0
var int legMinX = 0
if na(legMax) or msSOs != msSOs[1]
    legMax := high
    legMin := low
    legMaxX := bar_index
    legMinX := bar_index
else
    if high > legMax
        legMax := high
        legMaxX := bar_index
    if low < legMin
        legMin := low
        legMinX := bar_index


// ═══════════════ 4b. THE BIAS SOURCE — STRUCTURE, AND ONLY STRUCTURE ═════════
// There used to be five alternatives behind a dropdown here: EMA cross,
// Supertrend, Slope, Range midpoint and MTF EMA align. They were added to be
// measured, they were measured, and none of them beat the structure engine:
//
//   ../measurements/UNDERTOW_BIAS_SOURCE.md   EMA, Supertrend, Slope, Range
//                                             midpoint — none beat the baseline
//   ../measurements/UNDERTOW_MTF_EMA.md       two timeframes aligned — the
//                                             second one is worth -0.005 to
//                                             +0.020 R per trade
//   ../measurements/UNDERTOW_HTF.md           a higher-timeframe gate —
//                                             indistinguishable from discarding
//                                             the same number of trades at random
//
// So they are GONE FROM THE CHART, and with them seven inputs that group 1 did
// not need: the dropdown, two Supertrend settings, three MTF settings and
// `matureBars`, which only ever meant anything for a source that has no BOS to
// count. Every one of them is still in the Python port, because the studies
// above have to keep reproducing — see deploy/undertow-port-check.py, which
// lists them and says why.
//
// The `b*` names below are kept rather than inlined. Everything downstream
// reads them, they now say exactly one thing, and leaving the seam in place is
// cheaper than touching six hundred lines that are not wrong.

// ── the mux. Everything below reads these. ──────────────────────────────────
int   bOs         = msOs
bool  bChoch      = msChoch
bool  bBosUp      = msBosUp
bool  bBosDn      = msBosDn
int   bSOs        = msSOs
bool  bMinorChoch = msMinorChoch
float bMax        = msMax
float bMin        = msMin
int   bMaxX       = msMaxX
int   bMinX       = msMinX


// ══════════════════════════ 5. BIAS — THE THREE STATES ═══════════════════════
// None · Immature · Running · Ending. Only Immature and Running may trade.
//
// Every test below is true at a bar CLOSE. "Ending" in particular must never be
// a hindsight label: if a trend were only knowable as ending once the next one
// began, the chart would paint it correctly on history and the live scanner
// could never reproduce it.

var bool biasSeen = false
var int bosN = 0
var bool ending = false

if bChoch
    biasSeen := true
    bosN := 0
    ending := false

int biasDir = bOs == 1 ? 1 : -1
bool bosNow = biasDir > 0 ? bBosUp : bBosDn

if bosNow
    bosN := bosN + 1
    // A break in the trend's own direction is the trend proving itself again,
    // so it clears a previous Ending rather than leaving it latched forever.
    ending := false

// TWO ending conditions, from four. `endSweep`, `endStale` and the ADX gate
// came off the chart: all three shipped OFF, none was ever measured switched
// ON, and the panel's own Ending row never had cause to name them. They are
// still in the port for the studies that set them.
// They will sometimes call Ending while the trend keeps running — that is the
// right direction to be wrong in, because a false Ending costs a skipped setup
// and a missed one costs a loss.
bool minorAgainst = biasDir > 0 ? bSOs == 0 : bSOs == 1
bool endA = endMinor == "off" ? false : endMinor == "while opposed" ? minorAgainst : (bMinorChoch and minorAgainst)

// RETRACE DEPTH — the strongest chop guard available, and it needs no extra
// indicator. The impulse is the leg the engine has already tracked since the
// CHoCH: bMin to bMax. Give back enough of it and the trend is over in any
// practical sense, whatever the major structure still says.
//
// This exists because the chart showed a LONG reading "running" after price had
// retraced a 3,000-point impulse in full. The major structure was not wrong —
// it had not broken its last swing low — it was just useless as a trade bias by
// then, and every setup taken under it was a counter-trend trade wearing a
// with-trend label.
float msLeg = bMax - bMin
float retraced = msLeg > 0 ? (biasDir > 0 ? (bMax - close) / msLeg : (close - bMin) / msLeg) : 0.0
bool endD = retraceMax > 0 and retraced >= retraceMax / 100.0

// WHICH gate ended it, latched at the moment it latches. The panel used to say
// only that the bias killed a setup, and on three charts that was the single
// largest bucket — larger than the entries. "Which of the five" is the whole
// question, and one string answers it.
var string endWhy = "—"
if biasSeen and (endA or endD)
    if not ending
        endWhy := endA ? "minor" : "retrace"
    ending := true

string biasState = not biasSeen ? "none" : ending ? "ending" : bosN == 0 ? "immature" : "running"
bool tradeable = biasSeen and not ending


// ═══════════════════════ 6. THE PULLBACK, AND THE PIN ════════════════════════

// THE PULLBACK begins at the most recent structural extreme in the trend's
// direction — in a downtrend, the low that ended the last impulse leg. Its
// extreme is tracked as a RUNNING value, never the final one, so the pin test
// below is true at the pin's own close. A rule phrased around the swing high
// would need several more bars to confirm and would draw pins on history that
// no live scanner could have known about.
var float pbExt = na
var int pbExtX = na
var int pbStartX = na

bool pbReset = biasDir != nz(biasDir[1], biasDir) or (biasDir < 0 and bMinX != nz(bMinX[1], bMinX)) or (biasDir > 0 and bMaxX != nz(bMaxX[1], bMaxX))

if pbReset or na(pbExt)
    pbExt := biasDir < 0 ? high : low
    pbExtX := bar_index
    pbStartX := bar_index
else if biasDir < 0 and high >= pbExt
    pbExt := high
    pbExtX := bar_index
else if biasDir > 0 and low <= pbExt
    pbExt := low
    pbExtX := bar_index

// The stop buffer, in price. Called here and unconditionally: a ta.* inside a
// branch grows a different history.
float atrBuf = stopBuf * ta.atr(14)

// THE PIN. Size is irrelevant, body size is irrelevant. Three tests.
float rng = high - low
float upW = rng > 0 ? (high - math.max(open, close)) / rng : 0.0
float dnW = rng > 0 ? (math.min(open, close) - low) / rng : 0.0
float bodyR = rng > 0 ? math.abs(close - open) / rng : 0.0
bool isGreen = close >= open

// 1. FAMILY — which wick dominates, by a margin. The margin IS the doji
//    exclusion: without it, dn 0.21 against up 0.20 passes as a hammer while
//    looking exactly like the balanced bar being excluded.
bool famHam = dnW - upW >= wickEdge
bool famStar = upW - dnW >= wickEdge
bool famOk = (famHam and useHammer) or (famStar and useStar)
// STRICT: only the priority shape is admitted at all. Folded into famOk rather
// than added as a sixth gate because it IS a family test — useHammer and
// useStar are the same question asked without reference to the bias. It also
// keeps the funnel honest: a bar the strict rule refuses never counted as a
// pin, which is what nRaw is supposed to mean.
if famStrict and not (biasDir < 0 ? famHam : famStar)
    famOk := false

// 2. COLOUR — counter-trend. The candle's own direction is ignored: the bias
//    decides the trade, the pin only supplies the levels. So in a downtrend
//    BOTH green families qualify, hammer and inverted hammer alike.
bool colourOk = biasDir < 0 ? isGreen : not isGreen

// 3. LOCATION — the pin must BE the pullback extreme as of its own close, or
//    within `locTol` bars of it. At 0 this rejected roughly 6 in 10 of the
//    colour-correct pins, which makes it the strictest gate in the chain.
// THE ANCHOR. Under "trend extreme" the pin is looked for at the running
// extreme in the TREND's own direction — the leg low in a downtrend — which
// the bias block already tracks as bMinX / bMaxX.
int anchorRaw = pinAt == sAnchTrend ? (biasDir < 0 ? bMinX : bMaxX)
  : pinAt == sAnchLeg ? (biasDir < 0 ? legMinX : legMaxX) : pbExtX
int anchorX = na(anchorRaw) ? bar_index : anchorRaw
bool locOk = bar_index - anchorX <= locTol

bool barOk = barstate.isconfirmed
bool pinRaw = barOk and famOk
bool pinFound = pinRaw and tradeable
bool pinOk = pinFound and colourOk and locOk

// The grade is a LABEL and gates nothing. Free to record, and if partials later
// behave differently we can already tell them apart.
// WHY THIS BAR WAS TURNED DOWN, per bar. The panel's "last reject" is one
// string for the whole chart, which cannot answer "why not THAT candle" — the
// first question anyone asks of a detector.
// COLOUR IS TESTED BEFORE THE BIAS HERE, and the order is the whole point of
// the message. A green candle in an uptrend is not "the bias was ending" — it
// is the wrong candle, and reporting the bias for it puts an orange mark on
// every green bar in a bull trend, which is noise pretending to be a finding.
// The FUNNEL's order (bias, then colour) is unchanged; this is the
// explanation's order, and they answer different questions.
// THE STRICT REASON IS TESTED FIRST, and separately from the other family
// reasons, because "no dominant wick" on a textbook hanging man is a lie the
// debug layer would otherwise tell on every rejected bar with the toggle on.
string pinWhy = famStrict and (biasDir < 0 ? famStar : famHam) ? (biasDir < 0 ? "star family in a downtrend" : "hammer family in an uptrend") + " — 'priority shape only' is on" : not famOk ? "no dominant wick (doji, or the family is off)" : not colourOk ? (isGreen ? "green" : "red") + " in a" + (biasDir < 0 ? " downtrend" : "n uptrend") + " — wrong colour" : not tradeable ? "bias is " + biasState : not locOk ? str.tostring(bar_index - anchorX) + "b past the " + (pinAt == sAnchPull ? "pullback extreme" : pinAt == sAnchTrend ? "trend extreme" : "leg extreme") + " (tol " + str.tostring(locTol) + ")" : "ok"

string pinGrade = bodyR <= 0.35 ? "normal" : "partial"
string pinFace = famHam ? (isGreen ? "Hammer" : "Hanging man") : (isGreen ? "Inv hammer" : "Shooting star")
string pinCode = famHam ? (isGreen ? "HAM" : "HGM") : (isGreen ? "IH" : "SS")
string pinTag = pinFace + " · " + pinGrade + (math.min(upW, dnW) >= 0.05 ? " +wick" : "")


// ═══════════════════════════ 7. THE SETUP MACHINE ════════════════════════════
// EVERY QUALIFYING PIN GETS ITS OWN CANDIDATE, up to a cap. There is no
// "newest wins" or "first wins" any more, and removing that choice is the
// point.
//
// WHY. A pullback does not contain one pin, it contains several — each new
// extreme on the way down (or up) is another. The first version kept exactly
// one, which forced an arbitrary choice with no good answer:
//
//   newest wins   every new extreme replaced the pending candidate. On a real
//                 chart that was 256 supersessions against 14 survivors. Worse:
//                 in a long bias the bar that closes BELOW the pin's low — one
//                 of the two confirmations — is itself a new pullback low, so
//                 when it is also a pin it destroys the candidate it was
//                 confirming. A candidate survived mainly when the confirming
//                 bar happened NOT to be a pin, which is a strange thing to
//                 select on.
//   first wins    locks onto the first pin of the pullback, which is almost
//                 never the extreme, so its three levels are the wrong ones.
//
// Neither is the rule. The rule is that any of those pins might be the one that
// works, and v1 exists to find out which — so they all run, independently, each
// with its own three lines and its own window, and the chart shows which ones
// pay. The cap keeps the drawing budget and the chart readable; a pin arriving
// at the cap is turned away and counted, not silently dropped.
//
//   candidate  ->  armed  ->  filled
//        \           \
//         dropped     expired
//
// Nothing beyond `filled` is tracked. v1 scores no outcomes.

var array<Cand> cands = array.new<Cand>()
// Filled setups, walked forward to their target or their stop. This is the one
// place v1 scores anything, and it does so because the alternative — counting
// by eye off the chart — is worse, not because the number means much. See the
// tooltip on the panel row.
var array<Cand> trades = array.new<Cand>()

// ── the two questions the overlap drawing asks ───────────────────────────────
// Both read the LIVE trade array, which is why they live here and not beside
// the draw functions in section 2: Pine will not let a function reach forward
// to an array declared later in the file.
//
// Ghosts are excluded from both. A cancelled setup draws nothing, so it can
// neither lead a group nor make a real trade look crowded.

// How many real trades in this direction are ALREADY running? drawFill is
// called before the new candidate is pushed, so this counts the others and
// never itself. 0 means this fill leads its group and draws in full.
openSameDir(bool short) =>
    int n = 0
    if overlapMode == "collapse" and trades.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to trades.size() - 1
            Cand t = trades.get(i)
            if not t.ghost and t.short == short
                n := n + 1
    n

// Is another trade in the same direction still open, skipping index `skip`?
// The resolution loop removes as it goes, so the LAST of a group to resolve is
// the one that gets the full label and the rest get a glyph.
othersOpen(bool short, int skip) =>
    bool found = false
    if overlapMode == "collapse" and trades.size() > 1
        for i = 0 to trades.size() - 1
            Cand t = trades.get(i)
            if i != skip and not t.ghost and t.short == short
                found := true
    found

var int nRaw = 0
var int nPins = 0
var int nColour = 0
var int nLoc = 0
var int nCap = 0
var int nArmed = 0
var int nFilled = 0
// Armed and then lost, split by HOW — because the split is the whole point.
// "no return" is the one that sizes the OB/FVG work: the setup was valid, the
// limit was placed, and price simply never came back to it.
var int nMissBack = 0
var int nMissStop = 0
var int nMissGone = 0
var int nMissBias = 0
// The scoreboard. See the tooltip on its panel row: this is in-sample, on
// whatever settings happen to be loaded, and it is not evidence of anything.
var int nWon = 0
var int nLost = 0
// SPLIT BY THE BIAS AT THE PIN, not by the bias now. A setup taken in an
// immature trend stays an immature-trend trade however the chart reads by the
// time it resolves; scoring it against today's state is how a panel tells you
// a story about a trend that did not exist when the trade was placed.
var int nWonImm = 0
var int nLostImm = 0
var int nWonRun = 0
var int nLostRun = 0
// THE FEE, IN R, SUMMED OVER CLOSED REAL TRADES. It cannot be a flat number
// per trade: the cost is charged in price and the trade is scored in R, so a
// wider stop is a smaller cost. Accumulated at close, against the risk the
// trade actually carried — the same expression the port uses.
var float sumFee = 0.0
var string lastReject = "—"

// ── THE GHOST COLUMN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The bias gate cancels a triggered setup more often than it lets one through:
// 39 of 62 no-entries on one chart, 25 of 34 on another, 13 of 15 on a third.
// That is the largest single lever in the machine and nothing here measured it,
// because a cancelled setup was deleted and its counterfactual never existed.
//
// So a cancelled setup is no longer deleted. It is marked `ghost` and walked to
// the same fill, the same target and the same stop, into counters of its own.
// It draws nothing, holds no live slot, and cannot touch the real totals. If
// the ghosts lose, the gate is earning its place; if the ghosts win, the gate
// is throwing away the trades.
//
// It is still in-sample and it still proves nothing on its own. What it does is
// turn "should a triggered setup survive a bias flip" from an opinion into a
// number that can be pre-registered and tested out of sample.
var int gWon = 0
var int gLost = 0
var int gOpen = 0
// …and WHICH gate did the cancelling, since they are five different rules with
// one shared symptom.
var int nEndMinor = 0
var int nEndRetr = 0

// ── every live candidate, oldest first, removed in place when it resolves ────
if barOk and cands.size() > 0
    for i = cands.size() - 1 to 0
        Cand c = cands.get(i)
        bool gone = false
        bool filled = false
        // Per-candidate, not the global `lastReject`: several candidates can
        // resolve on one bar and a shared string would label each of them with
        // the last one's reason.
        string why = ""
        if not tradeable and not c.ghost
            why := "bias turned " + biasState + " · " + endWhy
            if c.armed
                nMissBias := nMissBias + 1
                nEndMinor := nEndMinor + (endWhy == "minor" ? 1 : 0)
                nEndRetr := nEndRetr + (endWhy == "retrace" ? 1 : 0)
                // Counted as a no-entry exactly as before — the real totals do
                // not change. Then kept, as a ghost, to find out what it would
                // have done. Drawn once, now, because after this it is invisible.
                if showUnfilled
                    drawMiss(c, why)
                // Said here, because the bottom of the loop deliberately lets
                // no ghost write to it and this is the drop worth reporting.
                lastReject := why
                c.ghost := true
            else
                gone := true
        if not gone and not c.armed
            // THE PULLBACK CAN STILL EXTEND while a candidate waits, and its
            // stop has to follow. Tracked per candidate rather than read from
            // pbExt, because pbExt RESETS when price makes a new structural
            // extreme — which is exactly what the Failure close often does —
            // and the reset would throw away the level the stop needs.
            if c.short and high > c.pbExt
                c.pbExt := high
            if not c.short and low < c.pbExt
                c.pbExt := low
            bool wHit = c.workHi ? beyondUp(c.hi, kWork) : beyondDn(c.lo, kWork)
            bool fHit = c.workHi ? beyondDn(c.lo, kFail) : beyondUp(c.hi, kFail)
            if wHit and not c.workOk
                c.workOk := true
                c.workBar := bar_index
                c.order := c.order == 0 ? 1 : c.order
            if fHit and not c.failOk
                c.failOk := true
                c.order := c.order == 0 ? 2 : c.order
            // THE ARMING EVENT IS THE FAILURE BREAK, and only one that lands
            // STRICTLY AFTER a working break. v1 armed on "both, in either
            // order", which was a misreading of the strategy: the pin is a
            // counter-trend candle, W is it appearing to WORK as a reversal
            // and F is that reversal FAILING, so the attempt has to happen
            // first. Either-order admits bars where the reversal never worked.
            //
            // STRICTLY after, because a bar spanning both lines gives no
            // intrabar order — the same reason a bar spanning entry and stop
            // counts as the loss. That bar does not arm; a later F does, which
            // is what makes F→W→F work.
            bool ready = confirmOrder == "either order" ? (c.workOk and c.failOk) : (fHit and c.workBar >= 0 and bar_index > c.workBar)
            if ready
                // The stop uses values CONFIRMED at or before this bar. A stop
                // drawn from a swing confirmed later is a stop that did not
                // exist when the trade was taken.
                float sw = c.short ? msSTopY : msSBtmY
                float base = stopSrc == sPin ? (c.short ? c.hi : c.lo) : stopSrc == sPull ? c.pbExt : stopSrc == sSwing ? sw : na
                float st = c.short ? base + atrBuf : base - atrBuf
                float risk = math.abs(st - c.focus)
                bool sane = not na(st) and risk > 0 and (c.short ? st > c.focus : st < c.focus)
                if sane
                    c.armed := true
                    c.armBar := bar_index
                    c.stop := st
                    c.target := c.short ? c.focus - rr * risk : c.focus + rr * risk
                    nArmed := nArmed + 1
                    // FIRST TO CONFIRM WINS. The pattern completed here, so
                    // the rest of this pullback's candles stop being separate
                    // ideas and their unarmed candidates go. Armed ones are
                    // untouched — they have levels and an order behind them.
                    //
                    // Walked BACKWARDS and skipping `i`, because this is
                    // inside the loop that is itself walking `cands`
                    // backwards: removing an index above the cursor is safe,
                    // removing the cursor is not.
                    if armWins and cands.size() > 1
                        for k = cands.size() - 1 to 0
                            if k != i
                                Cand y = cands.get(k)
                                if not y.armed and not y.ghost and y.short == c.short
                                    cands.remove(k)
                else
                    why := "stop is not beyond the entry"
                    gone := true
            else if bar_index - c.bar >= confirmBars
                why := "confirm window · no " + (not c.workOk ? "W" : confirmOrder == "either order" ? "F" : "F after the W")
                gone := true
        // THE PULLBACK IS OFTEN NOT FINISHED WHEN A SETUP ARMS, and freezing
        // the stop there sets it on an unfinished move. A long cannot arm
        // without a close below the pin's low, so this is not an edge case, it
        // is the normal case: the chart that produced this showed two pins with
        // the same entry, one stopped at 77,568 and one surviving at 77,432,
        // and the only difference was which bar they happened to arm on.
        //
        // Nothing is lost by moving a stop on an unfilled order — there is no
        // position. The costs are real but different: risk grows, the target
        // moves out with it, and "stop taken before the fill" can no longer
        // happen, because a stop below the running low cannot be run. Only the
        // pullback source tracks; the other two are fixed levels by definition.
        if not gone and c.armed and stopTrack and stopSrc == sPull
            bool deeper = c.short ? high > c.pbExt : low < c.pbExt
            if deeper
                c.pbExt := c.short ? high : low
                c.stop := c.short ? c.pbExt + atrBuf : c.pbExt - atrBuf
                float rk2 = math.abs(c.stop - c.focus)
                // Recomputed, or rr would quietly stop meaning rr.
                c.target := c.short ? c.focus - rr * rk2 : c.focus + rr * rk2
        // bar_index > c.armBar: the limit is only placed once the arming bar
        // has CLOSED, so a fill inside that same bar is look-ahead — the order
        // was not resting while it traded.
        if not gone and c.armed and bar_index > c.armBar
            bool tgtGone = c.short ? low <= c.target : high >= c.target
            bool stopHit = c.short ? high >= c.stop : low <= c.stop
            bool touched = high >= c.focus and low <= c.focus
            // THE MOVE HAPPENED WITHOUT US. If price reaches the TARGET while
            // the limit is unfilled, the whole trade was available and the
            // order was not on. Found on a real chart: price ran from the entry
            // past the target, came back, filled, and collapsed through the
            // stop, and a clean "2R" was drawn over it. Checked BEFORE the
            // fill, because the bar that returns to the entry is often the same
            // bar coming back from the target.
            if tgtGone
                why := "target reached before the fill"
                nMissGone := nMissGone + (c.ghost ? 0 : 1)
                gone := true
            // Stop next. When one bar spans both, which came first is
            // unknowable intrabar, so the setup is dropped rather than counted.
            else if stopHit
                why := "stop taken before the fill"
                nMissStop := nMissStop + (c.ghost ? 0 : 1)
                gone := true
            else if touched
                nFilled := nFilled + (c.ghost ? 0 : 1)
                gOpen := gOpen + (c.ghost ? 1 : 0)
                filled := true
                if not c.ghost
                    drawFill(c, openSameDir(c.short))
                // Kept, to be walked to its target or its stop below. `c` is
                // about to leave `cands`, but the object survives the removal.
                trades.push(c)
                gone := true
            else if bar_index - c.armBar >= fillBars
                why := "fill window — price never came back"
                nMissBack := nMissBack + (c.ghost ? 0 : 1)
                gone := true
        if gone
            // A ghost is already counted, already drawn and already explained.
            // It must not write over the live panel's reason or draw a second ×.
            if why != "" and not c.ghost
                lastReject := why
            if c.armed and not filled and showUnfilled and not c.ghost
                drawMiss(c, why)
            cands.remove(i)

// ── filled trades, walked to their target or their stop ─────────────────────
// Checked from the FILL BAR onward, stop first. Both of those are the
// conservative reading: a limit filled intrabar really was exposed to the rest
// of that bar, and when one bar spans both levels the order is unknowable, so
// it counts as the loss.
if barOk and trades.size() > 0
    for i = trades.size() - 1 to 0
        Cand t = trades.get(i)
        bool lost = t.short ? high >= t.stop : low <= t.stop
        bool won = t.short ? low <= t.target : high >= t.target
        bool imm = t.state == "immature"
        bool run = t.state == "running"
        float tRisk = math.abs(t.focus - t.stop)
        float tFee = t.ghost or tRisk <= 0 ? 0.0 : feeFrac * t.focus / tRisk
        if lost
            nLost := nLost + (t.ghost ? 0 : 1)
            nLostImm := nLostImm + (t.ghost or not imm ? 0 : 1)
            nLostRun := nLostRun + (t.ghost or not run ? 0 : 1)
            sumFee := sumFee + tFee
            gLost := gLost + (t.ghost ? 1 : 0)
            gOpen := gOpen - (t.ghost ? 1 : 0)
            if not t.ghost
                drawResult(t, false, othersOpen(t.short, i))
            trades.remove(i)
        else if won
            nWon := nWon + (t.ghost ? 0 : 1)
            nWonImm := nWonImm + (t.ghost or not imm ? 0 : 1)
            nWonRun := nWonRun + (t.ghost or not run ? 0 : 1)
            sumFee := sumFee + tFee
            gWon := gWon + (t.ghost ? 1 : 0)
            gOpen := gOpen - (t.ghost ? 1 : 0)
            if not t.ghost
                drawResult(t, true, othersOpen(t.short, i))
            trades.remove(i)

// ── a new pin ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
if pinRaw
    nRaw := nRaw + 1
if pinFound
    nPins := nPins + 1
    if colourOk
        nColour := nColour + 1
    if colourOk and locOk
        nLoc := nLoc + 1
    if not colourOk
        lastReject := "colour · " + (isGreen ? "green" : "red") + " in a" + (biasDir < 0 ? " downtrend" : "n uptrend")
    else if not locOk
        lastReject := bar_index - anchorX > locTol ? "location · " + str.tostring(bar_index - anchorX) + "b past " + (pinAt == sAnchTrend ? "the leg extreme" : "extreme") + " (tol " + str.tostring(locTol) + ")" : "location"

if pinOk
    // THE NEWEST PIN SUPERSEDES, AND THE PRIORITY SHAPE OUTRANKS RECENCY.
    // The strategy's author, three times: in a bearish trend the candle
    // working against it is the GREEN HAMMER and in a bullish one the RED
    // SHOOTING STAR; the other shape is usable but second choice.
    //
    // So a new pin clears the unarmed candidates in its own direction —
    // EXCEPT that a non-priority shape does not displace a priority one
    // that is still waiting. An armed candidate is never touched: it has
    // levels and an order behind it and is not somebody's second opinion.
    //
    // Ported from the port's block of the same name, which is the only
    // copy that had it. UNDERTOW_V3.md measured the pair at nothing;
    // it ships because it is the rule, on the same footing as W→F.
    bool isPriority = biasDir < 0 ? famHam : famStar
    bool held = false
    if not isPriority and cands.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to cands.size() - 1
            Cand x = cands.get(i)
            if not x.armed and not x.ghost and x.short == (biasDir < 0)
                held := held or (x.workHi == (biasDir < 0))
    if not held and cands.size() > 0
        for i = cands.size() - 1 to 0
            Cand x = cands.get(i)
            if not x.armed and not x.ghost and x.short == (biasDir < 0)
                cands.remove(i)
    // THE CAP IS COUNTED AFTER THE SUPERSEDE, and it used to be counted
    // before. The order is not cosmetic: the pin above usually DROPS its
    // unarmed rivals, so counting the pool first turns a pin away because of
    // candidates it was about to delete — and then the chart draws nothing
    // while the port, which has always counted after, arms the setup.
    //
    // That was a real three-way split. The bot matched this file and both
    // disagreed with the port at the shipped cap of 4; the parity test could
    // not see it because it ran only at 64, where the pool never reaches the
    // cap and all three agree. The port's order is the one kept: every
    // measurement ran on it, and refusing a pin over rivals it deletes is
    // wrong on its own terms.
    //
    // The count is of LIVE candidates only. A ghost still sits in `cands` so
    // the one resolution loop can walk it, but it is not a setup any more and
    // must not be able to turn a real one away.
    int nLive = 0
    if cands.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to cands.size() - 1
            if not cands.get(i).ghost
                nLive := nLive + 1
    if nLive >= maxLive
        nCap := nCap + 1
        lastReject := "at the live cap (" + str.tostring(maxLive) + ")"
    else
        // WORKING IS THE DIRECTION THE CANDLE'S SHAPE PREDICTS, and Failure is
        // the opposite. They are NOT fixed to high and low.
        cands.push(Cand.new(bar = bar_index, hi = high, lo = low, focus = open,
          workHi = famHam, short = biasDir < 0, pbExt = pbExt, tag = pinTag,
          code = pinCode, state = biasState))


// ═══════════════════════════════ 8. DRAWING ══════════════════════════════════
// The two functions above (drawFill, drawMiss) are declared in section 2 with
// the rest of the helpers, because Pine reads top-down and the loop calls them.
// What is left here is the LIVE view and the structure.

// THE LIVE CANDIDATES. A fixed pool, created once and repositioned, so watching
// several setups develop costs nothing against the drawing budget and nothing
// is left behind when they resolve. This is the moment worth seeing: a setup
// that never fills still answers "was the pin right", which is the only
// question v1 exists to answer.
var array<line> lvLn = array.new<line>()
var array<label> lvLb = array.new<label>()
if barstate.isfirst
    for i = 0 to maxLive * 3 - 1
        lvLn.push(line.new(na, na, na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_index, width = 1))
    for i = 0 to maxLive - 1
        lvLb.push(label.new(na, na, "", xloc = xloc.bar_index,
          style = label.style_label_left, size = size.normal))

for i = 0 to maxLive - 1
    lvLn.get(i * 3).set_color(color(na))
    lvLn.get(i * 3 + 1).set_color(color(na))
    lvLn.get(i * 3 + 2).set_color(color(na))
    lvLb.get(i).set_color(color(na))
    lvLb.get(i).set_textcolor(color(na))

// `slot`, not `i`: `cands` now also holds ghosts, which draw nothing, so the
// array index is no longer the pool index — and the pool is only maxLive * 3
// lines wide, so using `i` would run off the end the first time a ghost existed.
int slot = 0
if cands.size() > 0
    for i = 0 to cands.size() - 1
        Cand c = cands.get(i)
        if not c.ghost and slot < maxLive
            color lc = c.short ? colShort : colLong
            line a = lvLn.get(slot * 3)
            line b = lvLn.get(slot * 3 + 1)
            line f = lvLn.get(slot * 3 + 2)
            // The WORKING line carries the direction colour and the Failure
            // line does not, so which is which reads without the settings.
            a.set_xy1(c.bar, c.hi)
            a.set_xy2(bar_index, c.hi)
            a.set_color(c.workHi ? lc : colLine)
            a.set_style(line.style_solid)
            b.set_xy1(c.bar, c.lo)
            b.set_xy2(bar_index, c.lo)
            b.set_color(c.workHi ? colLine : lc)
            b.set_style(line.style_solid)
            f.set_xy1(c.bar, c.focus)
            f.set_xy2(bar_index, c.focus)
            f.set_color(lc)
            f.set_style(line.style_dashed)
            label lb = lvLb.get(slot)
            lb.set_xy(bar_index, c.focus)
            lb.set_color(color.new(lc, 25))
            lb.set_textcolor(color.white)
            lb.set_text(c.code + " " + (c.armed ? "ARMED " + (c.order == 1 ? "W→F" : "F→W→F") : c.workOk ? "W✓ F·" : c.failOk ? "W· F✓" : "W· F·") + " " + str.tostring(bar_index - c.bar) + "b")
            slot := slot + 1

// THE SWING DOTS ARE GONE. They marked every confirmed pivot with a circle,
// which at length 50 would be a handful of dots saying less than the CHoCH and
// BOS lines below already say. LuxAlgo ships its own swing points OFF for the
// same reason.
// THE BREAKS, and each one names the pivot it broke so the scan has a span.
// A CHoCH broke the swing pivot the engine is holding; a BOS broke the running
// extreme, which the engine had finished updating on the PREVIOUS bar — hence
// the [1]. The Piv objects carry their own bar index, so nothing has to be
// latched here any more: under the old engine `msTop` was an na-except-at-a-
// pivot series and needed catching as it went past.
float dTopY = pvHi.cur
int   dTopX = pvHi.bx
float dBtmY = pvLo.cur
int   dBtmX = pvLo.bx

bool brkUp = msChoch and msOs == 1
bool brkDn = msChoch and msOs == 0
int  brkUpX = na(dTopX) ? bar_index : dTopX
int  brkDnX = na(dBtmX) ? bar_index : dBtmX
int  bosUpX = na(msMaxX[1]) ? bar_index : msMaxX[1]
int  bosDnX = na(msMinX[1]) ? bar_index : msMinX[1]
// The INTERNAL tier's pivots, for its own lines and for the order blocks.
float iTopY = inHi.cur
int   iTopX = na(inHi.bx) ? bar_index : inHi.bx
float iBtmY = inLo.cur
int   iBtmX = na(inLo.bx) ? bar_index : inLo.bx

// CHoCH AND BOS ARE DRAWN AS THE BREAK ITSELF, not as a mark on the breaking
// bar — a horizontal line at the level that was taken out, running from the
// pivot that set it to the bar that broke it, with the name at its midpoint.
// The shape LuxAlgo's Smart Money Concepts uses, and it is better for one
// concrete reason: a diamond under a candle says "something happened here",
// while the line says WHICH LEVEL went and HOW FAR BACK it had stood. The
// second is the thing being read when a bias is checked by eye.
//
// Capped at keepN like every other buffer here. Pine's ceiling is 500 lines
// and 500 labels for the whole indicator, and the setups need most of it.
var array<line> msLineBuf = array.new<line>()
var array<label> msLabBuf = array.new<label>()

// SOLID for the swing tier, DASHED for the internal one, which is LuxAlgo's
// own convention and the right way round: the heavier line is the structure
// that decides the bias, the lighter one is the texture inside it.
msBreak(int fromX, float lvl, string tag, bool up, bool internal) =>
    color c = up ? colLong : colShort
    msLineBuf.push(line.new(fromX, lvl, bar_index, lvl,
      xloc = xloc.bar_index, color = c,
      style = internal ? line.style_dashed : line.style_solid,
      width = internal ? 1 : 2))
    msLabBuf.push(label.new(math.round(0.5 * (fromX + bar_index)), lvl, tag,
      xloc = xloc.bar_index, color = color(na), textcolor = c,
      style = up ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up,
      size = internal ? size.tiny : size.small))
    // TWO TIERS SHARE ONE BUDGET and the internal tier is far the noisier, so
    // the cap is generous rather than keepN: at length 5 a screen can hold
    // thirty internal breaks and the swing lines must not be the ones evicted.
    while msLineBuf.size() > 120
        line.delete(msLineBuf.shift())
    while msLabBuf.size() > 120
        label.delete(msLabBuf.shift())

if barOk and showStruct
    // SOLID for a CHoCH, DASHED for a BOS. The CHoCH is the event that sets
    // the direction and the BOS is the one that confirms it, so the heavier
    // line belongs to the heavier event.
    if brkUp
        msBreak(brkUpX, dTopY, "CHoCH", true, false)
    else if msBosUp
        msBreak(bosUpX, msMax[1], "BOS", true, false)
    if brkDn
        msBreak(brkDnX, dBtmY, "CHoCH", false, false)
    else if msBosDn
        msBreak(bosDnX, msMin[1], "BOS", false, false)
    // AND THE INTERNAL TIER, which is most of what a LuxAlgo chart shows. At
    // length 5 it fires many times between two swing breaks; drawing only the
    // swing tier left two labels on a screen that should carry thirty.
    if inUp
        msBreak(iTopX, iTopY, inChoch ? "CHoCH" : "BOS", true, true)
    if inDn
        msBreak(iBtmX, iBtmY, inChoch ? "CHoCH" : "BOS", false, true)

// DEBUG: one mark per pin, and hovering it says why. Blue took it, grey did
// not, and the tooltip carries the reason either way. A bar with NO mark at all
// failed the very first test: its wicks were balanced, so it is not a pin shape
// in the first place.
//
// 300, not keepN: this is for scrolling back and interrogating a candle you can
// see, so the cap has to cover a screen of them rather than the last few
// setups. Pine's own ceiling is 500 labels and debug is off by default.
var array<label> dbgBuf = array.new<label>()
// TWO TIERS OF MARK, and the split is by whether the CANDLE was right.
//
//   ALWAYS, once debug is on — the candles that ARE the ones being looked for:
//     blue circle     taken
//     orange circle   the right candle, and the bias was not tradeable
//
//   ONLY WITH `Show rejected pins` — the candles that were never eligible:
//     red cross       wrong colour for the bias
//     grey cross      right candle, too far from the anchor
//
// A GREEN CANDLE IN AN UPTREND IS NOT A REJECTED PIN, it is a bar. Marking
// every one of them said nothing and buried the marks that meant something,
// so colour now decides which tier a bar lands in rather than which colour
// the cross is.
bool dbgKeep = colourOk and (pinOk or not tradeable)
if dbgOn and pinRaw and (dbgKeep or dbgRejects)
    color dbgC = pinOk ? color.new(color.blue, 30)
      : not colourOk ? color.new(color.red, 45)
      : not tradeable ? color.new(color.orange, 25)
      : color.new(color.gray, 45)
    // A CIRCLE MEANS "this was the candle", cross means it was not. The bias
    // being off is not the candle's fault, so it keeps the circle.
    bool dbgRound = pinOk or (colourOk and not tradeable)
    // ON THE SIDE THE ANCHOR IS ON. Under the pullback anchor a short's pin
    // sits at a high and a long's at a low; under the trend and leg anchors it
    // is the other way round, because those look at the trend's own extreme.
    // Putting the mark on the wrong side of the bar is why they looked
    // scattered under the new anchors.
    bool atHigh = pinAt == sAnchPull ? biasDir < 0 : biasDir > 0
    dbgBuf.push(label.new(bar_index, atHigh ? high : low, "",
      xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = dbgRound ? label.style_circle
      : label.style_xcross, size = size.tiny, color = dbgC,
      tooltip = (pinOk ? pinTag + " · taken" : pinTag + "\n" + pinWhy)
      + "\nbias " + (biasDir > 0 ? "LONG" : "SHORT") + " · " + biasState))
    while dbgBuf.size() > 300
        label.delete(dbgBuf.shift())


// ════════════════ 8b. ORDER BLOCKS AND FAIR VALUE GAPS ══════════════════════
// DETECTION ADAPTED FROM LuxAlgo's "Smart Money Concepts", CC BY-NC-SA 4.0,
// https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/ — the same source
// ../port/smc.py already transcribes for the structure engine. What is
// borrowed is the METHOD, run on Undertow's own CHoCH and BOS events rather
// than on LuxAlgo's pivots.
//
// WHY IT WAS WORTH REPLACING WHAT WAS HERE. The old rule was "the last
// opposite-coloured candle before a close through its extreme" — one bar of
// context, no filter, a fixed-width box that never went away. LuxAlgo's is a
// different quality of thing on three counts:
//
//   1. IT IS ANCHORED TO A STRUCTURE BREAK. When a pivot is taken out, the
//      span from that pivot to the breaking bar is scanned and the block is
//      the most extreme bar in it. That is the candle the break came from,
//      not whichever bar happened to precede it.
//   2. THE EXTREME IS FILTERED. On a bar whose range is >= 2x ATR(200) the
//      high and low are SWAPPED before the scan, so a single violent candle
//      cannot claim the whole leg. This is the part that does the work.
//   3. IT IS MITIGATED. A block is deleted once price trades through it, so
//      what stays on the chart is only what is still unfilled.
//
// The fair value gaps get the same treatment: two half-boxes split at the
// midpoint, an optional auto threshold from the running mean body move, and
// removal on fill.
//
// DRAWING ONLY, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE STATUS OF THIS SECTION. Entering on
// these zones was measured twice and came off the chart —
// ../measurements/UNDERTOW_BACKUP_FILL.md and UNDERTOW_LATE_BACKUP.md. A
// better-drawn zone is a better DESCRIPTION, not evidence of an edge, and
// nothing below feeds a single number on the panel.
type Zone
    box   bx   = na
    box   bx2  = na
    float top  = na
    float btm  = na
    bool  bull = false

var array<Zone> obZones = array.new<Zone>()
var array<Zone> fvgZones = array.new<Zone>()

// How many of each survive on the chart. Not inputs: LuxAlgo ships 5 order
// blocks and ../SETTINGS.md is the argument against offering a dial for a
// number nobody can judge from a chart.
int OB_KEEP = 5
int FVG_KEEP = 20

// ta.* unconditionally — a ta.* inside a branch grows a different history.
float atr200 = ta.atr(200)
// THE VOLATILITY SWAP. `parsedHigh`/`parsedLow` are the bar's own high and low
// EXCEPT on a bar wider than 2x ATR, where they are exchanged. A 2x-range bar
// then scores as its opposite extreme and loses the scan below, which is how a
// single spike stops becoming the order block for a whole leg.
bool obWild = (high - low) >= 2 * atr200
float obPH = obWild ? low : high
float obPL = obWild ? high : low

zoneDrop(array<Zone> arr, int idx) =>
    Zone z = arr.get(idx)
    box.delete(z.bx)
    box.delete(z.bx2)
    arr.remove(idx)

// MITIGATION. A bearish block dies when price trades above its high, a bullish
// one when price trades below its low — the wick, not the close, which is
// LuxAlgo's "High/Low" setting and the stricter of its two.
if barOk and obZones.size() > 0
    for k = obZones.size() - 1 to 0
        Zone z = obZones.get(k)
        if (z.bull and low < z.btm) or (not z.bull and high > z.top)
            zoneDrop(obZones, k)

if barOk and fvgZones.size() > 0
    for k = fvgZones.size() - 1 to 0
        Zone z = fvgZones.get(k)
        if (z.bull and low < z.btm) or (not z.bull and high > z.top)
            zoneDrop(fvgZones, k)

// THE SCAN. Walk back `span` bars and return the offset of the most extreme
// FILTERED value — the highest obPH for a bearish break, the lowest obPL for a
// bullish one. Returns an offset from the current bar, so `high[off]` is it.
obPick(int span, bool bull) =>
    // STARTS AT OFFSET 1, NOT 0. LuxAlgo scans
    // `parsedHighs.slice(pivot.barIndex, bar_index)`, and that slice is
    // half-open — it excludes the breaking bar itself. Seeding `val` from
    // offset 0 let the breaking bar win the scan, which would put the order
    // block ON the candle that broke structure rather than in the leg behind
    // it. That is the opposite of what the zone is meant to mark.
    int best = 1
    float val = bull ? obPL[1] : obPH[1]
    for b = 2 to math.min(math.max(span, 1), 300)
        float v = bull ? obPL[b] : obPH[b]
        if bull ? v < val : v > val
            val := v
            best := b
    best

obPush(int span, bool bull) =>
    int off = obPick(span, bull)
    color c = bull ? colOBBull : colOBBear
    Zone z = Zone.new(box.new(bar_index - off, obPH[off], bar_index, obPL[off],
      xloc = xloc.bar_index, extend = extend.right, border_color = color(na),
      bgcolor = c), na, obPH[off], obPL[off], bull)
    obZones.unshift(z)
    while obZones.size() > OB_KEEP * 2
        zoneDrop(obZones, obZones.size() - 1)


// FROM THE INTERNAL TIER, which is LuxAlgo's own default: they ship internal
// order blocks ON and swing order blocks OFF. Anchoring these to the SWING
// tier instead gave a couple of enormous boxes per screen, because the span
// from a 50-bar pivot to its break is most of the visible chart and the
// "most extreme bar in the span" is then a near-meaningless pick. At length 5
// the span is a leg, which is what an order block is supposed to be.
if barOk and showOB
    if inUp
        obPush(bar_index - iTopX, true)
    if inDn
        obPush(bar_index - iBtmX, false)

// FAIR VALUE GAPS. Two boxes per gap, split at the midpoint, because a gap is
// filled from one side and the half that is gone should read as gone.
//
// THE AUTO THRESHOLD is LuxAlgo's: the running mean of the absolute body move
// as a fraction, doubled, so a gap has to be bigger than twice the average
// candle to count. Off by default — a filter nothing here has measured is a
// filter nobody should trust.
float fvgDelta = (close[1] - open[1]) / (open[1] * 100)
float fvgThr = zoneAuto ? ta.cum(math.abs(fvgDelta)) / bar_index * 2 : 0.0

// LuxAlgo's OWN FVG COLOURS, verbatim, not a tint of the order block's. A gap
// and a block are different objects and telling them apart at a glance is
// worth more than a tidy palette — which is presumably why LuxAlgo ships them
// as their own green and red rather than deriving them from anything.
color FVG_BULL = #00ff68
color FVG_BEAR = #ff0008

fvgPush(float top, float btm, bool bull) =>
    color base = bull ? FVG_BULL : FVG_BEAR
    color fill = color.new(base, 70)
    // THE BORDER IS WHAT DRAWS THE MID LINE. The gap is two half-boxes meeting
    // at its midpoint, so bordering both puts a line across the middle for
    // free — and the midpoint is the level worth seeing, because a gap that
    // has been filled halfway is the one deciding whether it holds.
    color edge = color.new(base, 40)
    float mid = math.avg(top, btm)
    int r = bar_index + fvgExtend
    box b1 = box.new(bar_index, top, r, mid, xloc = xloc.bar_index,
      border_color = edge, bgcolor = fill)
    box b2 = box.new(bar_index, mid, r, btm, xloc = xloc.bar_index,
      border_color = edge, bgcolor = fill)
    Zone z = Zone.new(b1, b2, top, btm, bull)
    fvgZones.unshift(z)
    while fvgZones.size() > FVG_KEEP
        zoneDrop(fvgZones, fvgZones.size() - 1)

if barOk and showFVG
    // [2] is the OLDER bar. Pine counts backwards, and getting this inverted
    // is what made the PORT's FVG detector silently find zero gaps until a
    // test asserted both zone types fire.
    bool fvgUp = low > high[2] and close[1] > high[2] and fvgDelta > fvgThr
    bool fvgDn = high < low[2] and close[1] < low[2] and -fvgDelta > fvgThr
    if fvgUp
        fvgPush(low, high[2], true)
    if fvgDn
        fvgPush(low[2], high, false)

// ═══════════════════════════ 9. THE STATUS PANEL ═════════════════════════════
// A STATUS panel, not a stats table. It says what the indicator is currently
// seeing. It scores no outcomes, because v1 has none.
//
// `last reject` is the whole reason it exists: "why was nothing found" is the
// only question a quiet detector ever raises, and a count alone cannot answer
// it.

// THE BIAS BADGE. The stats table is seventeen rows and most of the time the
// only one being read is the first. This is that row on its own, in the
// opposite corner, so the table can be turned off without losing the thing
// that decides whether a setup can exist at all.
var table biasTag = table.new(position.bottom_right, 1, 1, border_width = 0)
if barstate.islast and showBias
    color bbc = not biasSeen ? color.gray : ending ? color.orange
      : biasDir > 0 ? colLong : colShort
    table.cell(biasTag, 0, 0,
      (not biasSeen ? "NO BIAS" : (biasDir > 0 ? "LONG" : "SHORT"))
      + (biasSeen ? "  ·  " + str.upper(biasState) : ""),
      text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(bbc, 10),
      text_size = size.normal)

var table pnl = table.new(position.top_right, 2, 17, border_width = 1,
  frame_width = 1, frame_color = color.new(color.gray, 60))

// `tip` rather than a wider cell: the caveats belong ON the number they
// qualify, and the panel was already too wide. table.cell takes a tooltip.
row(int r, string k, string v, color c, string tip) =>
    table.cell(pnl, 0, r, k, text_color = color.new(color.gray, 20),
      text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_left, tooltip = tip)
    table.cell(pnl, 1, r, v, text_color = c, text_size = size.normal,
      text_halign = text.align_left, tooltip = tip)

if barstate.islast and showStats
    color bc = not biasSeen ? color.gray : ending ? color.orange : biasDir > 0 ? colLong : colShort
    string dir = not biasSeen ? "—" : biasDir > 0 ? "LONG" : "SHORT"
    int noEntry = nMissBack + nMissStop + nMissGone + nMissBias
    // Every resolved trade is exactly rr:1 by construction — the target is
    // recomputed whenever the stop moves — so the GROSS net is arithmetic, not
    // a sum of individual results. The fee is not: it depends on each trade's
    // own risk, so it is summed as the trades close and subtracted here.
    float netR = nWon * rr - nLost - sumFee
    row(0, "bias", dir + " · " + biasState, bc,
      "marks: HAM/HGM/IH/SS = the setup candle · ▲▼ entry · ✓✕ target/stop · × triggered but never entered · ● swing high/low · CHoCH and BOS name themselves")
    row(1, "pullback", (na(pbStartX) ? "—" : str.tostring(bar_index - pbStartX) + "b")
      + " · rt " + str.tostring(math.round(retraced * 100)) + "%", color.gray,
      "bars since the pullback began and how much of the impulse it has given back")
    row(2, "setups found", str.tostring(nLoc), color.gray,
      "candles that passed all four tests: a dominant wick, a tradeable bias, the counter-trend colour, and sitting at the pullback extreme. Nothing has been confirmed yet — these are candidates.")
    row(3, "triggered", str.tostring(nArmed), color.gray,
      "of those, the ones where price then closed beyond BOTH the pin's high and its low inside the confirm window, and a sane stop existed. This is where a limit order would go on.")
    row(4, "entered", str.tostring(nFilled), color.gray,
      "of those, the ones where price came back and touched the limit at the Focus line.")
    // `trades` HOLDS GHOSTS TOO, and counting them here was a real bug: the
    // panel read "28 / 58 · 1 open" while `entered` read 86, because the one
    // open trade was a GHOST — a setup the bias gate cancelled, already
    // reported on its own row. 28 + 58 + 1 = 87 against 86 entered is the
    // arithmetic that gave it away, and on the chart it looked like a live
    // position that had plainly already stopped out.
    int openReal = 0
    if trades.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to trades.size() - 1
            if not trades.get(i).ghost
                openReal := openReal + 1
    // Win rate needs its own break-even printed beside it or it means nothing:
    // at rr 3.5 a coin clears 22.2%, and a 30% win rate reads like a losing
    // strategy until you know that. The break-even rides in the LABEL rather
    // than the value because the label column is already the width of
    // "minor · retrace" — it costs nothing there, and unlike a tooltip
    // it survives a screenshot.
    int nRun = nWonRun + nLostRun
    int nImm = nWonImm + nLostImm
    int nRes = nWon + nLost
    // THE BREAK-EVEN LINE INCLUDES THE FEE, and until ../measurements/
    // UNDERTOW_V3.md it did not. 100/(1+rr) is the line BEFORE costs — 22.2%
    // at 3.5R, which is also what a driftless random walk returns. The fee
    // moves it: break-even solves w*rr - (1-w) - drag = 0, so it is
    // (1 + drag)/(1 + rr), where drag is the average per-trade cost IN R.
    // That is 23.5% on 15m and 22.9% on 1h at 7bp, because the same fee is a
    // bigger share of a tighter stop. A panel colouring 23.0% green was
    // calling a losing strategy a winning one, on every chart, for thirteen
    // studies' worth of pages.
    float feeR = nRes > 0 ? sumFee / nRes : 0.0
    float beRate = 100.0 * (1.0 + feeR) / (1.0 + rr)
    // The colour is the same comparison as the number: net R above zero after
    // costs is exactly a win rate above this line.
    row(5, "won / lost · be " + str.tostring(beRate, "#.#") + "%",
      nRes == 0 ? "—" : str.tostring(nWon) + " / " + str.tostring(nLost)
      + " · " + str.tostring(100.0 * nWon / nRes, "#.#") + "%"
      + (openReal > 0 ? " · " + str.tostring(openReal) + " open" : ""),
      netR > 0 ? colLong : netR < 0 ? colShort : color.gray,
      "entered trades walked forward to their target or their stop, stop first when one bar spans both, and the share of them that won.\n\nBREAK-EVEN AT " + str.tostring(rr, "#.#") + "R IS " + str.tostring(beRate, "#.#") + "%, AFTER THE ROUND-TRIP FEE. Before it the line would be " + str.tostring(100.0 / (1.0 + rr), "#.#") + "%, which is also what a driftless random walk returns — but the fee is charged in price and the trade is scored in R, so it costs " + str.tostring(feeR, "#.###") + "R on the trades taken here and the line moves up by that much. This percentage is only information relative to the line, and the colour is that comparison. Green is above it, red below.\n\nSet the round-trip fee to 0 under Levels to read it gross.\n\n`open` counts REAL trades only — a ghost still running is on the gate-cost row, not here, and won + lost + open always equals `entered`. IN-SAMPLE: same caveat as net R.")
    row(6, "  running", nRun == 0 ? "—" : str.tostring(nWonRun) + " / "
      + str.tostring(nLostRun) + " · "
      + str.tostring(100.0 * nWonRun / nRun, "#.#") + "%",
      nRun == 0 ? color.gray : 100.0 * nWonRun / nRun > beRate ? colLong : colShort,
      "trades taken while the bias had already broken structure at least once since its CHoCH. The state is the one at the PIN, not now. Coloured against break-even for this reward ratio, which is " + str.tostring(beRate, "#.#") + "% at " + str.tostring(rr, "#.#") + "R — a win rate below that loses money however healthy it looks.")
    row(7, "  immature", nImm == 0 ? "—" : str.tostring(nWonImm) + " / "
      + str.tostring(nLostImm) + " · "
      + str.tostring(100.0 * nWonImm / nImm, "#.#") + "%",
      nImm == 0 ? color.gray : 100.0 * nWonImm / nImm > beRate ? colLong : colShort,
      "trades taken on a fresh CHoCH, before any break of structure in the new direction. Same break-even line: " + str.tostring(beRate, "#.#") + "%. If these two rows differ by much on a few hundred trades it is worth a study, not a setting change — 'immature' and 'running' were never measured apart.")
    row(8, "net R", (netR >= 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(netR, "#.#"),
      netR > 0 ? colLong : netR < 0 ? colShort : color.gray,
      "won x " + str.tostring(rr, "#.#") + " minus lost, GROSS — no fees and no slippage, and both matter at this size. IN-SAMPLE AND NOT EVIDENCE: whatever settings are loaded, on the bars on screen, no pre-registration, no control. A filter picked by watching a number like this scored +0.089 in sample and -0.082 out elsewhere in this project. Read it to check the detection looks sane, never to choose settings.")
    row(9, "no entry", str.tostring(noEntry), color.gray,
      "triggered, then lost before the limit filled. The breakdown below is what decides how much work a backup fill is worth.")
    row(10, "  back·gone·bias·stop",
      str.tostring(nMissBack) + "·" + str.tostring(nMissGone) + "·"
      + str.tostring(nMissBias) + "·" + str.tostring(nMissStop), color.gray,
      "BACK = price never came back to the limit — the OB/FVG number, these are the ones a backup fill would have caught. GONE = the target printed before the fill, so the move was available at a worse price. BIAS = the trend turned Ending while waiting. STOP = price went beyond the planned stop first; this is 0 whenever the stop is set to follow the pullback, because a stop below the running low cannot be run.")
    // WHICH gate cancelled them. On the three charts that prompted this row,
    // BIAS was the largest no-entry bucket on all three — 39 of 62, 25 of 34,
    // 13 of 15 — and the panel could not say which rule did it.
    row(11, "  minor · retrace",
      str.tostring(nEndMinor) + " · " + str.tostring(nEndRetr), color.gray,
      "the BIAS number above, split by which Ending rule fired: minor structure · retrace %. It used to be five rules; the other three shipped off, were never measured on, and came off the chart — see ../SETTINGS.md. The larger number here names the setting to question first, and between them these two decide this strategy.")
    // THE GHOST COLUMN. See section 7: these are the cancelled setups, walked
    // forward anyway. This is the only row here that tests a RULE rather than
    // reporting a count.
    float gNetR = gWon * rr - gLost
    row(12, "  gate cost",
      nMissBias == 0 ? "—" : str.tostring(gWon) + " / " + str.tostring(gLost)
      + " · " + (gNetR > 0 ? "cost " + str.tostring(gNetR, "#.#")
      : "saved " + str.tostring(-gNetR, "#.#")) + "R"
      + (gOpen > 0 ? " · " + str.tostring(gOpen) + " open" : ""),
      nMissBias == 0 ? color.gray : gNetR > 0 ? colShort : colLong,
      "what the setups the bias gate CANCELLED would have done if it had been allowed to let them run: filled at the same limit, then walked to the same target or the same stop. They are in no number above and they never draw on the chart.\n\nSAVED means the gate is earning its place — the setups it killed were losers.\nCOST means the gate is throwing trades away, and this is how much.\n\nSame caveat as net R, doubled: in-sample, on whatever settings happen to be loaded, no control and no pre-registration. It is a reason to run a study, never a result.")
    row(13, "live", slot == 0 ? "—" : str.tostring(slot), color.gray,
      "candidates currently waiting for their confirmations. Ghosts are excluded — they hold no slot.")
    row(14, "last reject", lastReject, color.new(color.gray, 20),
      "why the most recent candle was turned down. Hover any grey × on the chart for that candle's own reason.")
    if dbgOn
        row(15, "funnel", str.tostring(nRaw) + "·" + str.tostring(nPins) + "·"
          + str.tostring(nColour) + "·" + str.tostring(nLoc) + "·"
          + str.tostring(nArmed) + "·" + str.tostring(nFilled), color.gray,
          "pin shape · bias tradeable · colour · location · triggered · entered. The biggest drop between two of these is where the detector is actually selecting.")
        row(16, "at cap", str.tostring(nCap), color.gray,
          "setups turned away because " + str.tostring(maxLive) + " were already live. If this is large, raise 'Live setups at once'.")
    else
        row(15, "", "", color.gray, "")
        row(16, "", "", color.gray, "")
